# Line-ending rules.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine, so by default Git rewrites LF to
# CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for source files and FATAL for anything
# read by offset.

# Runway database (tools/make-runways.py, from OurAirports).
# FIXED-WIDTH file, 17 bytes per record, that the flight-radar guest
# BINARY-SEARCHES by seeking to record * 17. A CRLF conversion would make
# every record 18 bytes: every seek would land mid-record and the lookup would
# return a neighbouring aerodrome, or nothing — silently, and only after a
# fresh clone on Windows, never on the machine that generated the file.
# The generator already writes it in binary mode for the same reason.
sdcard/stackchan-companion/runways.csv -text

# Fonts and firmware images: never touch their bytes.
*.vlw  -text
*.bin  -text

# Shell scripts: LF, ALWAYS, whatever the checkout platform.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true here, so without this line a Linux clone gets
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and the kernel refuses it with "bad interpreter: no
# such file or directory" — a message that names the interpreter, not the
# carriage return, so it reads as a missing bash. These files exist precisely
# so a Linux user has something that runs; shipping them unrunnable would be
# worse than not shipping them.
*.sh text eol=lf
